# Model Selection and Interpretation

The recommended model below is selected automatically from the held-out test results. No model name is hard-coded as the winner.

```{r final-model-selection, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}
best_model <- model_results[which.min(model_results$RMSE), ]

interpretability_note <- if (grepl(
  "Ordinary|Stepwise|Ridge|LASSO|Elastic|Principal|Partial",
  best_model$Model
)) {
  "Its fitted coefficients can be examined to understand the direction and relative size of each predictor's relationship with heating load."
} else if (grepl("Tree", best_model$Model)) {
  "Its decision rules and tree diagram provide a direct visual explanation of how predictor thresholds lead to different heating-load predictions."
} else if (best_model$Model == "Random Forest") {
  "It is less transparent than a single regression tree, but the variable-importance plot shows which predictors contribute most strongly to its predictions."
} else if (best_model$Model == "Cubist") {
  "Its rule-based linear models provide more interpretation than many black-box methods because the conditions and regression equations can be inspected."
} else {
  "It provides strong predictive performance, although its internal prediction process is less directly interpretable than a linear model or a small decision tree."
}

cat("## Recommended model\n\n")
cat(sprintf(
  "Based on the held-out test results, **%s** should be selected for this dataset. It produced the lowest RMSE (**%.3f heating-load units**), meaning that its predictions typically differed from the observed heating-load values by approximately %.3f units when larger errors were given extra weight. Its MSE was **%.3f**, its MAE was **%.3f heating-load units**, and its test-set R-squared was **%.3f**. Therefore, the model explains approximately **%.1f%%** of the variation in heating load in the test data.\n\n",
  best_model$Model,
  best_model$RMSE,
  best_model$RMSE,
  best_model$MSE,
  best_model$MAE,
  best_model$R2,
  100 * best_model$R2
))
cat(
  "RMSE was used as the primary selection criterion because all models were evaluated on the same unseen test observations and RMSE penalizes large prediction errors. MAE was reviewed as an easily interpreted average error, MSE confirmed the squared-error ranking, and R-squared measured explained variation. The model-comparison chart supports the decision visually: the selected model has the shortest RMSE bar. ",
  interpretability_note,
  " Together, the test metrics, visualization, and interpretability assessment provide the basis for the final selection.\n",
  sep = ""
)
```
